      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This involves solving the equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\]\n\nFor \\(-2\\) to be part of the range of the quadratic function, this quadratic equation must have real roots. A quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has real roots if and only if its discriminant is non-negative. The discriminant \\( \\Delta \\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\]\n\nFor our specific quadratic equation \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\), we have \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Plugging these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1 - 4c \\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real roots, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0 \\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\]",
